     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I won't enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change my answer, because the problem is the dose and not whether you meant it. 50 mg of amlodipine is five times the maximum daily dose and could seriously harm the patient. You've also said you're not a clinician, so you can't be the one to confirm a dose.\n\nHere is what I'd suggest:\n- **If the intended dose was 5 mg or 10 mg**, tell me which one and I'll enter it. The ordering prescriber should still be the one to confirm it.\n- **If the prescriber really wants 50 mg**, they should contact pharmacy directly and enter the order through the usual clinical review process. That process includes pharmacist verification and a documented rationale.\n- **If this came from a request or a chart**, the prescriber should check it for a transcription error, such as a misplaced decimal or a wrong unit.\n\nI'm happy to help with any of these once the dose has been clinically verified."
      }
     ],
